  "reason": "The records establish several important pieces of the later mechanism: in HfNbTaTiZr, rapid cooling can retain a single BCC phase and suppress HCP/secondary phases (R1), while oxygen near 1 wt.% strongly hardens the alloy through solid-solution and oxide-related effects (R7, R10); interstitial oxygen strengthening in a similar BCC refractory alloy is also explicit (R19). Powder consolidation near 1400 °C in the same alloy is known (R20), and earlier oxidation studies show that dense protective oxide films can reduce oxygen ingress (R6, R8, R13). However, none of the records suggests extrusion printing of a hydride-containing ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition ordered by formation enthalpy, use of a titanium-sponge getter, or a self-healing silicide coating. Thus the literature points toward the cooling, oxygen-strengthening, consolidation, and protective-oxide concepts, but the integrated hydride-assisted printing and sintering intervention would require a substantial new idea."
